module nupp.io.files

--[[
`nupp.io.files` reads what the filesystem knows about a name: whether it
resolves, what it refers to, what a directory contains, and where the platform
keeps a user's folders. It also moves and removes names.

```nupp:playground
local files = nupp.io.files
for _, entry in ipairs(assert(files.list("src"))) do
    print(entry.kind, entry.name)
end
```

A file's bytes move through the same `Reader` and `Writer` contracts a buffer
uses, so a parser written against [byte I/O](nupp.io) works over a file without
knowing one is there.

A path argument is a string or a [](nupp.io.path.Path); a path result is a
string. An operation that fails because of the environment answers nil or false
with a reason, and a malformed argument raises at the call site.

## Querying a name

`info` answers one record of attributes:

```nupp
local info, reason = files.info("nupp.lua")
assert(info, reason)
assert(info.kind == "file")
print(info.size, info.modified, info.readOnly)
```

`info` follows symbolic links, so it describes what a name refers to rather than
the name itself. `size` is a byte count, `modified` is seconds since the Unix
epoch with whatever fractional part the platform records, and `readOnly` is the
platform's write refusal rather than a permission model.

`exists`, `isFile` and `isDirectory` answer the same question without the record
and without a reason. A missing path is `false` rather than an error:

```nupp
if files.isDirectory(candidate) then
    return files.list(candidate)
end
```

`isSymlink` is the one query that does not follow, because following is what
would hide the answer:

```nupp
assert(files.createSymlink("target.txt", "alias"))
assert(files.isSymlink("alias"))
assert(files.isFile("alias"))
assert(files.readLink("alias") == "target.txt")
```

`createSymlink` takes an optional third argument, `"file"` or `"directory"`.
Only Windows distinguishes the two; elsewhere it is ignored.

## Listing a directory

`list` answers every entry in a directory, in one call:

```nupp
local entries, reason = files.list("src/nupp")
assert(entries, reason)
```

Each entry has a `name` without any directory part and a `kind` describing the
entry *itself*, so a link inside a listing reads as `symlink` rather than as
what it points at. The order is the platform's, which is not sorted.

The kind comes from the directory itself rather than a second query per name, so
listing a large directory costs one call.

## Matching paths

`glob` expands a pattern into sorted path strings. `*` and `?` stay within one
path component, `[abc]` and `[!abc]` select characters, and `**` crosses
directory boundaries. No matches is an empty list; an invalid pattern or a
filesystem error answers a reason.

```nupp
for _, path in ipairs(assert(files.glob("src/**/*.nupp"))) do
    print(path)
end
```

## Creating, moving and removing

These three operations move names rather than bytes, so each answers now:

```nupp
assert(files.createDirectory("out/lib/native"))
assert(files.rename("out/report.tmp", "out/report.json"))
assert(files.remove("out/stale", true))
```

`createDirectory` creates every missing parent, and an existing directory
succeeds, which is what a caller building a tree wants rather than a race with
its own earlier call. `rename` replaces an existing destination. `remove` takes
a file, a symbolic link, or an empty directory; the second argument removes a
directory's contents with it, and without it a populated directory answers a
reason.

`setReadOnly` sets or clears the write refusal that `info` reports.

## Temporary names

`createTemporaryFile` and `createTemporaryDirectory` both take options and
answer a path:

```nupp
local scratch, reason = files.createTemporaryDirectory({prefix = "build-"})
assert(scratch, reason)
```

The file or directory is *created*, not proposed, so no second caller can take
the name between the answer and the use. `directory` selects where, defaulting
to the platform's temporary directory; `prefix` and `suffix` bracket the
generated part, which is what puts an extension on a temporary file.

A temporary is an [owner](docs/learn/runtime/ownership/index.md): closing it removes
what it created, and the checker runs that cleanup at the end of the scope
whether the block falls through, returns early, or raises.

```nupp
do
    local scratch = assert(files.createTemporaryFile({suffix = ".json"}))
    assert(files.write(scratch:toString(), encoded))
    assert(scratch:persist("out/report.json"))
end
```

`persist` moves it somewhere permanent and discharges the obligation, so the
close that follows does nothing. That pair is the reason to make one: write to a
name nobody else can take, then put it where it belongs, so a reader never sees
a half-written file under the final name.

## Reading and writing a whole file

`read` and `write` move a file's complete contents in one call:

```nupp
local text, reason = files.read("nupp.lua")
assert(text, reason)
assert(files.write("out/report.json", encoded))
```

`append` adds to the end and creates a missing file. `copy` duplicates one path
over another. `writeAtomic` writes through a temporary beside the destination
and renames over it, so an interrupted write leaves the destination as it was
rather than half replaced. A failed write removes the temporary rather than
leaving it behind.

A NUL byte is content, not a terminator, in every direction.

```nupp
for line in assert(files.lines("access.log")) do
    print(line)
end
```

`lines` closes the file when it reaches the end. A trailing carriage return is
removed, so a file written on either platform reads the same. Abandoning the
iterator early leaves the file open until it is collected; open it yourself when
you mean to stop. A read that fails once iteration has begun raises, because the
iterator has no failure channel to answer through.

## Reading and writing through a cursor

`open` hands over a `File` and the obligation to close it:

```nupp
do
    local file = assert(files.open("image.png"))
    local reader = file:newReader()
    print(reader:read(8))
end
```

The reader and writer satisfy `nupp.io.Reader` and `nupp.io.Writer`, so `read`,
`readSpan`, `readInto`, `transferTo`, `write`, `writeSpan` and `flush` mean
what they mean over a buffer. Native callers can read and write through checked
spans without separating a pointer from its bound. `readInto` commits the
destination buffer only after a successful read, and `transferTo` streams a
file of any size through a fixed window:

```nupp
do
    local source = assert(files.open("input.bin"))
    local sink = assert(files.open("output.bin", "w"))
    print(source:newReader():transferTo(sink:newWriter()))
end
```

`mode` is `r`, `w`, `a`, or the update modes `r+`, `w+` and `a+`, matching C and
Lua. `seek(offset, origin)` moves the cursor, with `origin` one of `set`,
`current` or `end`; `position` and `size` answer where it is and how long the
file is. A reader or writer over a closed file answers a reason rather than
raising.

## Platform directories

Two queries answer where the process is and where the platform keeps a user's
folders:

```nupp
print(assert(files.currentDirectory()))
print(assert(files.userFolder("documents")))
```

`userFolder` takes `home`, `documents`, `downloads`, `desktop`, `pictures`,
`music` or `videos`. It resolves from the environment: the `XDG_*` variables
where they are set, and the platform's conventional names under the home
directory otherwise. A desktop that records its folders somewhere else is not
consulted, and a folder that does not exist answers a reason rather than a path
that is not there.

## Waiting

A whole-file `read`, `write`, `append`, `writeAtomic` or `copy` settles on a
worker thread rather than on yours, and the call waits for it by suspending.
What that means depends on where the call runs, and on nothing the call says:

| Where it runs | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| an ordinary program | it sleeps, driving the readiness pump |
| under an installed handler | it parks, and the handler resumes it |
| inside a `nosuspend` region | it is refused, at compile time |

One call site covers all three. A library that reads a file works inside a game
frame and inside a command-line program without knowing which it is in, and a
transfer that settled before it was observed never reaches any of this.

::: deepdive Names answer now, bytes go to a worker lane
Anything that moves names rather than bytes answers now, because the platform
answers it now. Anything that moves a whole file's bytes is submitted to the
native library's worker lane and waited for, so a program inside a scheduler
yields instead of blocking its host. Making both suspend would tax every `stat`
for a wait that never happens; making neither suspend would block a host on a
transfer it cannot see. See [suspension.md](docs/learn/runtime/concurrency/suspension.md)
for how one call takes either path.
:::

The immediate operations cannot suspend, so a region that forbids waiting still
permits asking what a path is, listing a directory, or renaming one:

```nupp
nosuspend do
    if files.isDirectory(candidate) then
        print(#assert(files.list(candidate)))
    end
    print(files.read(candidate)) -- NUPP2701: `read` may suspend
end
```

## Cost

Reaching `nupp.io.files` selects the compiler-owned Rust provider, whose filesystem
operations use the platform standard library and whose blocking transfers run through
the shared native executor, and loads it on first use. A
program that never reaches it links nothing and initializes nothing, which is
the rule for every facility in the [standard
library](docs/learn/runtime/data/standard-library.md). A target that uses it also carries
the suspension runtime, because that is what answers the wait above.

The lane those workers run is bounded three ways: how many transfers may be
live, how many bytes they may hold between them, and how large one may be. Past
any of the three a submission answers a reason rather than queueing, because a
queue that grows with its callers eventually takes the process with it.
`pendingTransfers` answers what the lane is holding.

Metadata, listings and cursor reads through an open `File` do not use the lane.
Scheduling a transfer costs more than those cost to run.

::: seealso
- [](nupp.io.path) for building and taking apart the names these operations
  read
- [](nupp.io) for the buffers, readers and writers a file's bytes move through
- [ownership.md](docs/learn/runtime/ownership/borrowing.md) for the complete contract an
  open file and a temporary path are written against
- [suspension.md](docs/learn/runtime/concurrency/suspension.md) for what a wait does under a
  scheduler, and how several compose
:::
]]

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The native binding
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

const native = require("nupp.runtime.native")
const suspension = require("nupp.suspension")
const paths = require("nupp.io.path")
const io = require("nupp.io")
const span = require("nupp.mem.span")

-- The record is declared here rather than in the runtime cdef below, because
-- `ffi.new` names it and the checker has to know the type to accept that. It reaches
-- LuaJIT as a bare `struct` rather than a typedef, which is why the prototype below
-- writes it that way. The functions stay in the runtime declaration: they are reached
-- through the handle `nupp.runtime.native` opened, not the default namespace a `cdef
-- function` binds to.
cdef struct NuppNativeV2FilesInfo
    kind: uint32
    read_only: int32
    size: uint64
    modified: number
end

cdef struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice
    data: cstring
    length: uint64
end

native.ffi.cdef[[
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesInfo(
    struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice, int32_t, struct NuppNativeV2FilesInfo *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesReadLink(struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice, uint64_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesCreateSymlink(
    struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice, struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice, int32_t);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesSetReadOnly(struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice, int32_t);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesCreateDirectory(struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesRemove(struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice, int32_t);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesRename(
    struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice, struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesList(struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice, uint64_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesGlob(struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice, uint64_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesCreateTemporary(
    struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice, struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice,
    struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice,
    int32_t, uint64_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesCurrentDirectory(uint64_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesUserFolder(uint32_t, uint64_t *);

int32_t nuppNativeV2FileOpen(struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice, uint32_t, uint64_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FileRead(uint64_t, uint8_t *, size_t, size_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FileWrite(uint64_t, const uint8_t *, size_t, size_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FileSeek(uint64_t, int64_t, uint32_t, int64_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FileSize(uint64_t, int64_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FileFlush(uint64_t);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FileRelease(uint64_t);

int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesTransferSubmitRead(
    struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice, uint64_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesTransferSubmitWrite(
    struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice, struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice,
    uint32_t, uint64_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesTransferSubmitCopy(
    struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice, struct NuppNativeV2FilesSlice, uint64_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesTransferStatus(uint64_t, uint32_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesTransferTakeBytes(uint64_t, uint64_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesTransferCancel(uint64_t);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesTransferRelease(uint64_t);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesTransferPoll(size_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesTransferWait(uint64_t, size_t *);
int32_t nuppNativeV2FilesTransferPending(size_t *);
]]

local ffi = native.ffi
local C = native.C
const FILES_FEATURE = 128
local featureChecked = false

local function requireFiles(): nil
    if not featureChecked then
        native.requireFeature(FILES_FEATURE, "files")
        featureChecked = true
    end
end

local function slice(text: string): any
    requireFiles()
    local value = ffi.new<NuppNativeV2FilesSlice>()
    value.data = text
    value.length = #text

    return value
end

local function fileRead(handle: any, borrows destination: uint8[?], count: integer, wrote: any): integer
    local call = C.nuppNativeV2FileRead as function(
        handle: any,
        borrows destination: uint8[?],
        count: integer,
        wrote: any
    ): integer

    return call(handle, destination, count, wrote)
end

local function fileWrite(handle: any, borrows source: const uint8[?], count: integer, wrote: any): integer
    local call = C.nuppNativeV2FileWrite as function(
        handle: any,
        borrows source: const uint8[?],
        count: integer,
        wrote: any
    ): integer

    return call(handle, source, count, wrote)
end

-- The library numbers what a path is, what an entry is, which user folder, how a file
-- is opened, and what a seek is measured from. Each of these is its side of one of
-- those enumerations.
local KINDS = {[1] = "file", [2] = "directory", [3] = "other", [4] = "symlink",}
local ENTRIES = {f = "file", d = "directory", l = "symlink", o = "other",}
local FOLDERS = {home = 0, documents = 1, downloads = 2, desktop = 3, pictures = 4, music = 5, videos = 6,}
local MODES = {r = 0, w = 1, a = 2, ["r+"] = 3, ["w+"] = 4, ["a+"] = 5,}
local ORIGINS = {set = 0, current = 1, ["end"] = 2,}

local READ_SIZE: integer = 65536
local PENDING: integer = 0
local READY: integer = 1
local FAILED: integer = 2
local CANCELED: integer = 3

-- The readiness pump this module registers with whoever is handling suspensions. The
-- priority puts it with the other completion-oriented sources rather than ahead of
-- them; nothing here is latency-critical.
local SOURCE, PRIORITY = "nupp-files", 20

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- What the filesystem answers with
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

--- What a resolved path refers to. A `symlink` answer only ever comes from
--- `isSymlink`, since every other operation follows the link first.
--- @export
export type Kind = "file" | "directory" | "symlink" | "other"

--- A well-known user folder. Resolved from the environment, so a desktop that records
--- its folders elsewhere is not consulted.
--- @export
export type UserFolder = "home" | "documents" | "downloads" | "desktop" | "pictures" | "music" | "videos"

--- How an open file may be used. The three update modes read and write: `r+` needs an
--- existing file, `w+` truncates one, and `a+` appends.
--- @export
export type Mode = "r" | "w" | "a" | "r+" | "w+" | "a+"

--- What a seek offset is measured from.
--- @export
export type Origin = "set" | "current" | "end"

--- Answers each line in turn, and nil at the end of the file.
--- @export
export type LineIterator = function(): string?

--- One resolved path's attributes.
--- @export
export record Info
    --- What the path refers to, after following symbolic links.
    kind: Kind

    --- The byte length of a file's contents.
    size: integer

    --- Seconds since the Unix epoch, with a fractional part where the platform
    --- records one.
    modified: number

    --- Whether the platform refuses writes to this path.
    readOnly: boolean
end

--- One directory child, as the directory itself describes it.
--- @export
export record Entry
    --- The child's name, without any directory part.
    name: string

    --- What the entry itself is, without following a symbolic link.
    kind: Kind
end

--- Where a temporary is created, and what surrounds its generated name.
--- @export
export record TemporaryOptions
    --- Where to create it, or the platform's temporary directory when omitted.
    directory: (string | paths.Path)?

    --- Text before the generated part.
    prefix: string?

    --- Text after the generated part, such as an extension.
    suffix: string?
end

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Owners and argument checks
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- A terminal has to be a declared function wherever a contract names it, and these
-- records hand each other out: an open file answers a reader and a writer. Written one
-- per record, each terminal would have to follow the record it takes and precede the
-- record that hands it out, and no such order exists. One generic terminal over the
-- shape they all share is written once, ahead of them.
local interface Owner
    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: Owner): nil
end

--- Releases any of this module's owners. Every one of them is handed out under this
--- exact cleanup, which is why a caller never names it.
local function destroyOwner<T is Owner>(takes self: T): nil
    self:drop()
end

-- Declared ahead of the records because their methods reach them, and defined below
-- because they name the records. The types are the contract either way.
local newReaderOver: function(file: File): FileReader
local newWriterOver: function(file: File): FileWriter
local scratch: function(borrows self: FileReader, count: integer): any
local usable: function(borrows self: FileReader): (File?, string?)
local writable: function(borrows self: FileWriter): (File?, string?)

--- @raises when value is neither a path nor a string
local function named(value: any, what: string, level: integer): string
    if type(value) == "string" then
        return value as string
    end
    if type(value) == "table" and (value as any).toString ~= nil then
        return (value as paths.Path):toString()
    end
    error("nupp: io.files " .. what .. " must be a path or a string", level)
end

--- @raises when value is not an integer
local function whole(value: any, what: string, level: integer): integer
    if type(value) ~= "number" or value ~= math.floor(value as number) then
        error("nupp: io.files " .. what .. " must be an integer", level)
    end

    return value as integer
end

--- @raises when value is not a non-negative integer
local function counted(value: any, what: string, level: integer): integer
    if whole(value, what, level + 1) < 0 then
        error("nupp: io.files " .. what .. " must not be negative", level)
    end

    return value as integer
end

--- @raises when value is not a positive integer
local function positive(value: any, what: string, level: integer): integer
    local answer = whole(value, what, level + 1)
    if answer < 1 then
        error("nupp: io.files " .. what .. " must be positive", level)
    end

    return answer
end

-- A direct call answers a status, and a failure's reason lives on the provider's
-- thread-local error channel.
local function done(status: integer): (boolean, string?)
    if status == 0 then
        return true, nil
    end

    return false, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
end

-- Dynamic answers cross as generic v2 byte handles. `native.bytes` copies their
-- contents into a Lua string and releases the generational handle exactly once.
local function answer(status: integer, output: any): (string?, string?)
    if status ~= 0 then
        return nil, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
    end

    return native.bytes(output[0]), nil
end

-- Reads through a caller-owned native buffer. FileReader and the line iterator
-- share this crossing; the iterator owns its file rather than borrowing a view.
local function readBytes(handle: any, into: any, count: integer): (string?, string?)
    local output = ffi.new("size_t[1]")
    local status = C.nuppNativeV2FileRead(handle, into, count, output)
    if status ~= 0 then
        return nil, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
    end
    local got = tonumber(output[0]) as integer
    if got == 0 then
        return "", nil
    end

    return ffi.string(into, got), nil
end

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open files
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

--- An open file, and the obligation to close it.
--- @export
export record File
    private _handle: any
    private _closed: boolean

    --- Opens a forward-only reader at the file's current position.
    --- @param self this file
    --- @return the new reader
    --- @raises when the file is closed
    function newReader(self): FileReader
        if self._closed then
            error("nupp: io.files File is closed", 2)
        end

        return newReaderOver(self)
    end

    --- Opens a forward-only writer at the file's current position.
    --- @param self this file
    --- @return the new writer
    --- @raises when the file is closed
    function newWriter(self): FileWriter
        if self._closed then
            error("nupp: io.files File is closed", 2)
        end

        return newWriterOver(self)
    end

    --- Answers the file's byte length without moving the cursor.
    --- @param self this file
    --- @return the byte length, or nil on failure
    --- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
    --- @raises when the file is closed
    function size(self): (integer?, string?)
        if self._closed then
            error("nupp: io.files File is closed", 2)
        end
        local output = ffi.new("int64_t[1]")
        local status = C.nuppNativeV2FileSize(self._handle, output)
        if status ~= 0 then
            return nil, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
        end

        return tonumber(output[0]) as integer, nil
    end

    --- Moves the cursor and answers where it landed.
    --- @param self this file
    --- @param offset the distance to move
    --- @param origin what the offset is measured from, defaulting to the start
    --- @return the new position, or nil on failure
    --- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
    --- @raises when origin names no seek origin
    function seek(self, offset: integer?, origin: Origin?): (integer?, string?)
        if self._closed then
            error("nupp: io.files File is closed", 2)
        end
        local whence = ORIGINS[origin or "set"]
        if whence == nil then
            error("nupp: io.files has no seek origin named " .. tostring(origin), 2)
        end
        local output = ffi.new("int64_t[1]")
        local status = C.nuppNativeV2FileSeek(self._handle, whole(offset or 0, "seek offset", 2), whence, output)
        if status ~= 0 then
            return nil, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
        end

        return tonumber(output[0]) as integer, nil
    end

    --- Answers the cursor's current position.
    --- @param self this file
    --- @return the position, or nil on failure
    --- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
    function position(self): (integer?, string?)
        return self:seek(0, "current")
    end

    --- Pushes buffered writes at the operating system.
    --- @param self this file
    --- @return whether the flush succeeded
    --- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
    --- @raises when the file is closed
    function flush(self): (boolean, string?)
        if self._closed then
            error("nupp: io.files File is closed", 2)
        end

        return done(C.nuppNativeV2FileFlush(self._handle))
    end

    --- Whether this file has been closed.
    --- @param self this file
    --- @return whether it is closed
    function isReleased(self): boolean
        return self._closed
    end

    --- Closes the file as an ownership terminal.
    ---
    --- Repeated calls are safe. Declared rather than written inline because the
    --- cleanup contract needs the `nosuspend`, and an inline method cannot carry
    --- one.
    --- @param self this file, spent by the call
    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: File): nil

    --- Closes the file and reports whether it succeeded.
    ---
    --- Repeated calls are safe.
    --- @param self this file, spent by the call
    --- @return whether the close succeeded
    function close(takes self): boolean
        self:drop()

        return true
    end
end

function File.drop(takes self: File): nil
    local state = unsafe release self
    if state._closed then
        return
    end
    state._closed = true
    local handle = state._handle
    state._handle = nil
    C.nuppNativeV2FileRelease(handle)
end

-- Closes a file without consuming an owner, which is what the line iterator needs: it
-- holds a file the caller never sees and closes it from inside a closure.
local function closeFile(file: File): nil
    if file._closed then
        return
    end
    file._closed = true
    local handle = file._handle
    file._handle = nil
    C.nuppNativeV2FileRelease(handle)
end

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Readers and writers
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

--- A forward-only reader over an open file.
--- @export
export record FileReader is io.Reader
    private _file: any
    private _scratch: any
    private _capacity: integer
    private _closed: boolean

    --- Releases it as an ownership terminal.
    ---
    --- Repeated calls are safe. Declared rather than written inline: a cleanup
    --- contract is `nosuspend`, and an inline method cannot carry one.
    --- @param self this owner, spent by the call
    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: FileReader): nil

    --- Reads up to `count` bytes.
    ---
    --- An empty answer is the end of the file.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @param count the most bytes to read
    --- @return the bytes, or nil when the reader or its file is closed
    --- @return why it could not read, when unsuccessful
    --- @raises when count is not a positive integer
    function read(self, count: integer): (string?, string?)
        local file, reason = usable(self)
        if file == nil then
            return nil, reason
        end
        local wanted = positive(count, "FileReader:read count", 2)
        local into = scratch(self, wanted)

        return readBytes((file as File)._handle, into, wanted)
    end

    function readSpan(self, exclusive destination: span.Writable<uint8>): (integer?, string?)
        local file, reason = usable(self)
        if file == nil then
            return nil, reason
        end
        local wanted = positive(#destination, "FileReader:readSpan destination length", 2)
        local pointer = destination:ref()
        local output = ffi.new("size_t[1]")
        local status = fileRead((file as File)._handle, pointer, wanted, output)
        if status ~= 0 then
            return nil, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
        end

        return tonumber(output[0]) as integer, nil
    end

    --- Reads into a buffer.
    ---
    --- A zero answer is the end of the file.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @param destination the buffer to write into
    --- @param offset where in the destination to start, or the beginning
    --- @param count the most bytes to read
    --- @return how many bytes were read, or nil on failure
    --- @return why it could not read, when unsuccessful
    function readInto(self, exclusive destination: io.Buffer, offset: integer?, count: integer?): (integer?, string?)
        local file, reason = usable(self)
        if file == nil then
            return nil, reason
        end
        local from = counted(offset or 0, "FileReader:readInto offset", 2)
        local wanted = positive(count or READ_SIZE, "FileReader:readInto count", 2)
        local chunk, failure = self:read(wanted)
        if chunk == nil then
            return nil, failure
        end
        local taking = #(chunk as string)
        if taking == 0 then
            return 0, nil
        end
        local input = span.fromString(chunk as string)
        local lease = destination:reserveWrite(from, wanted)
        do
            local output = lease:span()
            for index = 1, taking do
                output[index] = input[index]
            end
            drop output
        end
        lease:commit(taking)

        return taking, nil
    end

    --- Reads the rest of the file into a writer.
    --- @param self this reader
    --- @param destination the writer to fill
    --- @return how many bytes moved, or nil on failure
    --- @return why it could not, when unsuccessful
    function transferTo(self, exclusive destination: io.Writer): (integer?, string?)
        local file, reason = usable(self)
        if file == nil then
            return nil, reason
        end
        local total: integer = 0
        while true do
            local chunk, why = self:read(READ_SIZE)
            if chunk == nil then
                return nil, why
            end
            if #(chunk as string) == 0 then
                return total, nil
            end
            local wrote, failure = destination:write(chunk as string)
            if not wrote then
                return nil, failure
            end
            total = total + #(chunk as string)
        end
    end

    --- Closes the reader without closing the file behind it.
    --- @param self this reader, spent by the call
    function close(takes self): nil
        self:drop()
    end
end

--- An appending writer over an open file.
--- @export
export record FileWriter is io.Writer
    private _file: any
    private _closed: boolean

    --- Releases it as an ownership terminal.
    ---
    --- Repeated calls are safe. Declared rather than written inline: a cleanup
    --- contract is `nosuspend`, and an inline method cannot carry one.
    --- @param self this owner, spent by the call
    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: FileWriter): nil

    --- Writes bytes at the file's current position.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @param bytes the bytes to write
    --- @return whether they were written
    --- @return why they were not, when unsuccessful
    --- @raises when bytes is not a string
    function write(exclusive self, bytes: string): (boolean, string?)
        local file, reason = writable(self)
        if file == nil then
            return false, reason
        end
        if type(bytes) ~= "string" then
            error("nupp: io.files FileWriter:write needs a string", 2)
        end
        local output = ffi.new("size_t[1]")
        local status = C.nuppNativeV2FileWrite((file as File)._handle, bytes, #bytes, output)
        if status ~= 0 then
            return false, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
        end

        return true, nil
    end

    function writeSpan(exclusive self, borrows source: span.ByteSpan): (integer?, string?)
        local file, reason = writable(self)
        if file == nil then
            return nil, reason
        end
        local count = #source
        if count == 0 then
            return 0, nil
        end
        local pointer = source:ref()
        local output = ffi.new("size_t[1]")
        local status = fileWrite((file as File)._handle, pointer, count, output)
        if status ~= 0 then
            return nil, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
        end

        return tonumber(output[0]) as integer, nil
    end

    --- Pushes the file's buffered writes at the operating system.
    --- @param self this writer
    --- @return whether the flush succeeded
    --- @return why it did not, when it did not
    function flush(self): (boolean, string?)
        local file, reason = writable(self)
        if file == nil then
            return false, reason
        end

        return (file as File):flush()
    end

    --- Closes the writer without closing the file behind it.
    --- @param self this writer, spent by the call
    function close(takes self): nil
        self:drop()
    end
end

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Temporary paths
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

--- A created temporary path, and the obligation to settle it.
---
--- Closing removes it. `persist` moves it somewhere permanent instead and discharges
--- the obligation, which is the whole reason to make one: write to a name nobody else
--- can take, then put it where it belongs.
--- @export
export record TemporaryPath
    private _text: string
    private _directory: boolean
    private _closed: boolean

    --- Returns the created path.
    --- @param self this temporary path
    --- @return the path text
    function toString(self): string
        return self._text
    end

    --- Moves this path to a permanent destination, replacing what is there.
    --- @param self this temporary path
    --- @param destination where to move it
    --- @return whether the move happened
    --- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
    function persist(self, destination: string | paths.Path): (boolean, string?)
        if self._closed then
            return false, "the temporary path is released"
        end
        local to = named(destination, "destination path", 2)
        local moved, reason = done(C.nuppNativeV2FilesRename(slice(self._text), slice(to)))
        if not moved then
            return false, reason
        end
        self._closed = true

        return true, nil
    end

    --- Whether this path has been removed or persisted.
    --- @param self this temporary path
    --- @return whether it is settled
    function isReleased(self): boolean
        return self._closed
    end

    --- Removes the path as an ownership terminal.
    ---
    --- Repeated calls, and a call after `persist`, are safe and do nothing. Declared
    --- rather than written inline because the cleanup contract needs the `nosuspend`,
    --- and an inline method cannot carry one.
    --- @param self this temporary path, spent by the call
    drop: nosuspend function(takes self: TemporaryPath): nil

    --- Removes the path and reports whether it succeeded.
    ---
    --- Repeated calls, and a call after `persist`, are safe and do nothing.
    --- @param self this temporary path, spent by the call
    --- @return whether the removal succeeded
    function close(takes self): boolean
        local state = unsafe release self
        if state._closed then
            return true
        end
        state._closed = true

        return C.nuppNativeV2FilesRemove(slice(state._text), state._directory and 1 or 0) == 0
    end

    metamethod __tostring: function(self): string
end

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Dropping
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

function TemporaryPath.drop(takes self: TemporaryPath): nil
    local state = unsafe release self
    if state._closed then
        return
    end
    state._closed = true
    C.nuppNativeV2FilesRemove(slice(state._text), state._directory and 1 or 0)
end

    ;
(TemporaryPath as {[string]: any}).__tostring = function(self: TemporaryPath): string
    return self:toString()
end

function FileReader.drop(takes self: FileReader): nil
    local state = unsafe release self
    state._closed = true
    state._scratch = nil
    state._capacity = 0
end

function FileWriter.drop(takes self: FileWriter): nil
    local state = unsafe release self
    state._closed = true
end

newReaderOver = function(file: File): FileReader
    return new FileReader(_file = file, _scratch = nil, _capacity = 0, _closed = false)
end

newWriterOver = function(file: File): FileWriter
    return new FileWriter(_file = file, _closed = false)
end

-- Grown by doubling with a floor at one read, so a caller that asks for one byte at a
-- time does not reallocate for each of them.
scratch = function(borrows self: FileReader, count: integer): any
    local state = self as any
    if count > (state._capacity as integer) then
        local size = (state._capacity as integer) * 2
        if size < count then
            size = count
        end
        if size < READ_SIZE then
            size = READ_SIZE
        end
        state._scratch = ffi.new<uint8[?]>(size)
        state._capacity = size
    end

    return state._scratch
end

-- A reader and its file are closed independently, and either being closed is an
-- ordinary answer rather than a failure.
usable = function(borrows self: FileReader): (File?, string?)
    local state = self as any
    if state._closed then
        return nil, "the reader is closed"
    end
    local file = state._file as File
    if (file as any)._closed then
        return nil, "the file is closed"
    end

    return file, nil
end

writable = function(borrows self: FileWriter): (File?, string?)
    local state = self as any
    if state._closed then
        return nil, "the writer is closed"
    end
    local file = state._file as File
    if (file as any)._closed then
        return nil, "the file is closed"
    end

    return file, nil
end

--- Describes one path, following symbolic links unless asked not to.
local function described(path: string | paths.Path, follow: boolean, level: integer): any
    local text = named(path, "path", level + 1)
    local record = ffi.new<NuppNativeV2FilesInfo[1]>()
    if C.nuppNativeV2FilesInfo(slice(text), follow and 1 or 0, record) ~= 0 then
        return nil
    end
    local found: any = nil
    -- A pointer is not indexable in checked code, so the one-element array the library
    -- filled is read through its own index. The fields are copied out rather than the
    -- element handed back: an element is an interior reference that does not anchor
    -- its array, so returning one would let the storage it points into be collected
    -- under the caller.
    unsafe do
        found = {
            kind = record[0].kind,
            readOnly = record[0].read_only ~= 0,
            size = record[0].size,
            modified = record[0].modified,
        }
    end

    return found
end

-- Every whole-file transfer this program submitted and has not yet consumed. The
-- lane that runs them is bounded, so a program that submits more than it waits for is
-- something a caller can ask about.
local waits: {any} = {}

local function transferState(handle: any): (integer, string?)
    local output = ffi.new("uint32_t[1]")
    local status = C.nuppNativeV2FilesTransferStatus(handle, output)
    if status ~= 0 then
        return FAILED, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
    end

    local state = tonumber(output[0]) as integer
    if state == FAILED then
        return state, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
    end

    return state, nil
end

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Driving the request loop
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Moves every settled request off the wait list and resumes whoever was waiting for
-- it. Answers how many moved, which is what a readiness pump owes its driver.
local function harvest(): integer
    local moved: integer = 0
    local index = #waits
    while index > 0 do
        local entry = waits[index]
        if transferState(entry.handle) ~= PENDING then
            waits[index] = waits[#waits]
            waits[#waits] = nil
            moved = moved + 1
            entry.resume(true)
        end
        index = index - 1
    end

    return moved
end

local function polled(): integer
    local output = ffi.new("size_t[1]")
    C.nuppNativeV2FilesTransferPoll(output)

    return harvest()
end

local function slept(waitMs: integer): integer
    local output = ffi.new("size_t[1]")
    C.nuppNativeV2FilesTransferWait(waitMs, output)

    return harvest()
end

local function forget(entry: any): nil
    for index = 1, #waits do
        if waits[index] == entry then
            waits[index] = waits[#waits]
            waits[#waits] = nil

            return
        end
    end
end

-- A request that has already settled is not waited for at all, so the common small
-- read never reaches the suspension machinery.
local function await(handle: any): nil
    if transferState(handle) ~= PENDING then
        return
    end
    suspension.suspend("file transfer", function(resume: function(boolean), context: any): function()?
        local entry = {handle = handle, resume = resume,}
        context:source(SOURCE, PRIORITY, polled, slept)
        waits[#waits + 1] = entry
        -- Settled between the status check above and the subscription: resume now and
        -- answer no cancellation, which is what a subscription that did not park owes.
        if transferState(handle) ~= PENDING then
            forget(entry)
            resume(true)

            return nil
        end

        -- Cancellation raises out of the suspension, so `settled` never runs to
        -- destroy this handle; destroying it here is what gives the lane its
        -- request and byte budget back. The worker holds its own reference to the
        -- shared state, so a transfer already running is unaffected. An abandoned
        -- park is cancelled twice by design, and the first call frees the handle,
        -- so the whole body runs once and the second call finds nothing to do.
        local cancelled = false

        return function(): nil
            if cancelled then
                return
            end
            cancelled = true
            forget(entry)
            C.nuppNativeV2FilesTransferCancel(handle)
            C.nuppNativeV2FilesTransferRelease(handle)
        end
    end)
end

-- Waits for one submitted request and answers the handle when it is ready. A failed
-- request is destroyed here, because nothing else will read it.
local function settled(handle: any): (any?, string?)
    if handle == nil or handle == 0 then
        return nil, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
    end
    await(handle)
    local state, failure = transferState(handle)
    if state ~= READY then
        local reason = failure or (state == CANCELED and "file transfer was cancelled" or "file transfer failed")
        C.nuppNativeV2FilesTransferRelease(handle)

        return nil, reason
    end

    return handle, nil
end

local function transferred(handle: any): (boolean, string?)
    local ready, reason = settled(handle)
    if ready == nil then
        return false, reason
    end
    C.nuppNativeV2FilesTransferRelease(ready)

    return true, nil
end

local function fetched(handle: any): (string?, string?)
    local ready, reason = settled(handle)
    if ready == nil then
        return nil, reason
    end
    local output = ffi.new("uint64_t[1]")
    local status = C.nuppNativeV2FilesTransferTakeBytes(ready, output)
    if status ~= 0 then
        local failure = ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
        C.nuppNativeV2FilesTransferRelease(ready)

        return nil, failure
    end
    local out = native.bytes(output[0])
    C.nuppNativeV2FilesTransferRelease(ready)

    return out as string, nil
end

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Naming a temporary
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

--- @raises when value is neither a string nor a field of that name
local function optional(options: TemporaryOptions?, field: string, level: integer): string
    local value = options ~= nil and (options as any)[field] or nil
    if value == nil then
        return ""
    end
    if type(value) ~= "string" then
        error("nupp: io.files temporary " .. field .. " must be a string", level)
    end

    return value as string
end

--- @raises when the options name something that is not a path or a string
local function temporary(options: TemporaryOptions?, directory: boolean, level: integer): (string?, string?)
    local where = options ~= nil and (options as TemporaryOptions).directory or nil
    local root = where ~= nil and named(where, "temporary directory", level + 1) or ""
    local prefix = optional(options, "prefix", level + 1)
    local suffix = optional(options, "suffix", level + 1)

    local output = ffi.new("uint64_t[1]")
    local status = C.nuppNativeV2FilesCreateTemporary(
        slice(root),
        slice(prefix),
        slice(suffix),
        directory and 1 or 0,
        output
    )

    return answer(status, output)
end

--- @raises when value is neither bytes nor a byte view
local function payload(value: any, what: string, level: integer): string
    if type(value) == "string" then
        return value as string
    end
    if type(value) == "table" and (value as any).getString ~= nil then
        return (value as io.ByteView):getString()
    end
    error("nupp: io.files " .. what .. " must be bytes or a byte view", level)
end

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Asking about a path
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

--- Describes one path, following symbolic links.
---
--- #### Examples
---
--- Read a file's size:
---
--- ```nupp
--- local info = assert(nupp.io.files.info("nupp.lua"))
--- assert(info.kind == "file" and info.size > 0)
--- ```
--- @param path the path to describe
--- @return the attributes, or nil when the path cannot be read
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function info(path: string | paths.Path): (Info?, string?)
    local found = described(path, true, 2)
    if found == nil then
        return nil, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
    end

    return new Info(
        kind = KINDS[tonumber(found.kind)] or "other",
        size = tonumber(found.size) as integer,
        modified = found.modified as number,
        readOnly = found.readOnly as boolean
    ), nil
end

--- Whether a path resolves to anything at all.
--- @param path the path to test
--- @return whether it resolves
--- @export
export function exists(path: string | paths.Path): boolean
    return described(path, true, 2) ~= nil
end

--- Whether a path resolves to a regular file.
--- @param path the path to test
--- @return whether it is a file
--- @export
export function isFile(path: string | paths.Path): boolean
    local found = described(path, true, 2)

    return found ~= nil and found.kind == 1
end

--- Whether a path resolves to a directory.
--- @param path the path to test
--- @return whether it is a directory
--- @export
export function isDirectory(path: string | paths.Path): boolean
    local found = described(path, true, 2)

    return found ~= nil and found.kind == 2
end

--- Whether a path is itself a symbolic link, without following it.
--- @param path the path to test
--- @return whether it is a symbolic link
--- @export
export function isSymlink(path: string | paths.Path): boolean
    local found = described(path, false, 2)

    return found ~= nil and found.kind == 4
end

--- Reads a symbolic link's target without resolving it.
--- @param path the link to read
--- @return the target text, or nil on failure
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function readLink(path: string | paths.Path): (string?, string?)
    local text = named(path, "path", 2)
    local output = ffi.new("uint64_t[1]")

    return answer(C.nuppNativeV2FilesReadLink(slice(text), output), output)
end

--- Creates a symbolic link.
---
--- `kind` selects Windows's directory link and is ignored elsewhere, because only
--- Windows distinguishes the two.
--- @param target what the link points at
--- @param link where to create the link
--- @param kind the link kind, defaulting to a file link
--- @return whether the link was created
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @raises when kind names neither a file nor a directory link
--- @export
export function createSymlink(
    target: string | paths.Path,
    link: string | paths.Path,
    kind: ("file" | "directory")?
): (boolean, string?)
    local to = named(target, "symlink target", 2)
    local at = named(link, "symlink path", 2)
    if kind ~= nil and kind ~= "file" and kind ~= "directory" then
        error("nupp: io.files symlink kind must be 'file' or 'directory'", 2)
    end

    return done(C.nuppNativeV2FilesCreateSymlink(slice(to), slice(at), kind == "directory" and 1 or 0))
end

--- Sets or clears a path's read-only attribute.
--- @param path the path to change
--- @param readOnly whether to refuse writes
--- @return whether the attribute was set
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function setReadOnly(path: string | paths.Path, readOnly: boolean): (boolean, string?)
    local text = named(path, "path", 2)

    return done(C.nuppNativeV2FilesSetReadOnly(slice(text), readOnly and 1 or 0))
end

--- Creates a directory and every missing parent.
---
--- An existing directory succeeds.
--- @param path the directory to create
--- @return whether the directory exists afterwards
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function createDirectory(path: string | paths.Path): (boolean, string?)
    local text = named(path, "path", 2)

    return done(C.nuppNativeV2FilesCreateDirectory(slice(text)))
end

--- Removes a file, a symbolic link, or a directory.
--- @param path the path to remove
--- @param recursive whether to remove a directory's contents with it
--- @return whether the path was removed
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function remove(path: string | paths.Path, recursive: boolean?): (boolean, string?)
    local text = named(path, "path", 2)

    return done(C.nuppNativeV2FilesRemove(slice(text), recursive and 1 or 0))
end

--- Renames a path, replacing an existing destination.
--- @param from the path to rename
--- @param to the new path
--- @return whether the rename happened
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function rename(from: string | paths.Path, to: string | paths.Path): (boolean, string?)
    local source = named(from, "source path", 2)
    local destination = named(to, "destination path", 2)

    return done(C.nuppNativeV2FilesRename(slice(source), slice(destination)))
end

--- Lists a directory's immediate children.
---
--- #### Examples
---
--- Count the modules beside a file:
---
--- ```nupp
--- local entries = assert(nupp.io.files.list("src"))
--- for _, entry in ipairs(entries) do
---     print(entry.kind, entry.name)
--- end
--- ```
--- @param path the directory to list
--- @return the children in the platform's order, or nil on failure
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function list(path: string | paths.Path): ({Entry}?, string?)
    local text = named(path, "path", 2)
    local output = ffi.new("uint64_t[1]")
    local status = C.nuppNativeV2FilesList(slice(text), output)
    if status ~= 0 then
        return nil, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
    end
    -- The library answers one blob: a kind byte, the name, and a NUL, repeated.
    local blob = native.bytes(output[0])
    local entries: {Entry} = {}
    local at = 1
    while at <= #blob do
        local stop = blob:find("\0", at + 1, true) as integer
        entries[
            #entries + 1
        ] = new Entry(kind = ENTRIES[blob:sub(at, at)] or "other", name = blob:sub(at + 1, stop - 1))
        at = stop + 1
    end

    return entries, nil
end

--- Expands a filesystem pattern into matching paths.
---
--- `*` and `?` match within one path component, character classes use `[abc]` or
--- `[!abc]`, and `**` crosses directory boundaries. The returned paths are sorted, and
--- no matches is an empty list.
---
--- #### Examples
---
--- Walk every source file under a directory:
---
--- ```nupp
--- for _, match in ipairs(assert(nupp.io.files.glob("src/**/*.nupp"))) do
---     print(match)
--- end
--- ```
--- @param pattern the filesystem pattern to expand
--- @return the matching paths, or nil when the pattern or walk fails
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function glob(pattern: string | paths.Path): ({string}?, string?)
    local text = named(pattern, "glob pattern", 2)
    local output = ffi.new("uint64_t[1]")
    local status = C.nuppNativeV2FilesGlob(slice(text), output)
    if status ~= 0 then
        return nil, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
    end
    local blob = native.bytes(output[0])
    local matches: {string} = {}
    local at = 1
    while at <= #blob do
        local stop = blob:find("\0", at, true)
        if stop == nil then
            matches[#matches + 1] = blob:sub(at)
            break
        end
        matches[#matches + 1] = blob:sub(at, (stop as integer) - 1)
        at = (stop as integer) + 1
    end

    return matches, nil
end

--- Reads a whole file.
---
--- #### Examples
---
--- Read a file's bytes, or report why not:
---
--- ```nupp
--- local text, reason = nupp.io.files.read("nupp.lua")
--- assert(text, reason)
--- ```
--- @param path the file to read
--- @return the contents, or nil on failure
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function read(path: string | paths.Path): (string?, string?)
    local text = named(path, "path", 2)
    local output = ffi.new("uint64_t[1]")
    local status = C.nuppNativeV2FilesTransferSubmitRead(slice(text), output)

    if status ~= 0 then
        return nil, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
    end

    return fetched(output[0])
end

--- Writes a whole file, replacing its contents.
--- @param path the file to write
--- @param bytes the contents
--- @return whether the write succeeded
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function write(path: string | paths.Path, bytes: string | io.ByteView): (boolean, string?)
    local text = named(path, "path", 2)
    local out = payload(bytes, "contents", 2)
    local output = ffi.new("uint64_t[1]")
    local status = C.nuppNativeV2FilesTransferSubmitWrite(slice(text), slice(out), 0, output)

    if status ~= 0 then
        return false, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
    end

    return transferred(output[0])
end

--- Adds to the end of a file, creating it when it does not exist.
--- @param path the file to extend
--- @param bytes what to add
--- @return whether the write succeeded
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function append(path: string | paths.Path, bytes: string | io.ByteView): (boolean, string?)
    local text = named(path, "path", 2)
    local out = payload(bytes, "contents", 2)
    local output = ffi.new("uint64_t[1]")
    local status = C.nuppNativeV2FilesTransferSubmitWrite(slice(text), slice(out), 1, output)

    if status ~= 0 then
        return false, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
    end

    return transferred(output[0])
end

--- Writes a whole file through a temporary beside it, so an interrupted write leaves
--- the destination as it was rather than half replaced.
---
--- Prefer this to `write` wherever a reader may be looking at the destination
--- while it is being replaced, since `write` truncates in place.
--- @param path the file to write
--- @param bytes the contents
--- @return whether the write succeeded
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function writeAtomic(path: string | paths.Path, bytes: string | io.ByteView): (boolean, string?)
    local text = named(path, "path", 2)
    local out = payload(bytes, "contents", 2)
    local output = ffi.new("uint64_t[1]")
    local status = C.nuppNativeV2FilesTransferSubmitWrite(slice(text), slice(out), 2, output)

    if status ~= 0 then
        return false, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
    end

    return transferred(output[0])
end

--- Copies a file's contents over a destination.
--- @param from the file to copy
--- @param to where to copy it
--- @return whether the copy succeeded
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function copy(from: string | paths.Path, to: string | paths.Path): (boolean, string?)
    local source = named(from, "source path", 2)
    local destination = named(to, "destination path", 2)
    local output = ffi.new("uint64_t[1]")
    local status = C.nuppNativeV2FilesTransferSubmitCopy(slice(source), slice(destination), output)

    if status ~= 0 then
        return false, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
    end

    return transferred(output[0])
end

--- How many whole-file transfers this program is still holding.
---
--- Whole-file reads, writes and copies settle on worker threads, and the lane that
--- runs them is bounded. This answers what it holds, which is what a program that
--- submitted more than it consumed needs to see.
--- @return the number of live transfers
--- @export
export function pendingTransfers(): integer
    requireFiles()
    local output = ffi.new("size_t[1]")
    if C.nuppNativeV2FilesTransferPending(output) ~= 0 then
        return 0
    end

    return tonumber(output[0]) as integer
end

----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Opening and reading
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

--- Opens a file and hands over the obligation to close it.
---
--- #### Examples
---
--- Read a header without holding the whole file:
---
--- ```nupp
--- do
---     local file = assert(nupp.io.files.open("image.png"))
---     print(file:newReader():read(8))
--- end
--- ```
--- @param path the file to open
--- @param mode how it may be used, defaulting to reading
--- @return the open file, or nil on failure
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @raises when mode names no open mode
--- @export
export function open(path: string | paths.Path, mode: Mode?): (affine(File?, destroyOwner), string?)
    local text = named(path, "path", 2)
    local selected = MODES[mode or "r"]
    if selected == nil then
        error("nupp: io.files has no mode named " .. tostring(mode), 2)
    end
    local output = ffi.new("uint64_t[1]")
    local status = C.nuppNativeV2FileOpen(slice(text), selected, output)
    if status ~= 0 then
        return nil, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
    end

    return new File(_handle = output[0], _closed = false), nil
end

--- Iterates a file's lines, closing it at the end.
---
--- A trailing carriage return is removed, so a file written on either platform reads
--- the same. The iterator stops at the end of the file; abandoning it early leaves the
--- file open until it is collected.
--- @param path the file to read
--- @return an iterator answering each line, or nil on failure
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @raises when a read fails once iteration has begun, since the iterator has no
--- failure channel to answer through
--- @export
export function lines(path: string | paths.Path): (LineIterator?, string?)
    -- The file is opened here rather than through `open`, because `open` hands back an
    -- obligation and this function has nobody to hand it to: the iterator is what owns
    -- the file, and closes it when it reaches the end. Releasing an owner the caller
    -- never sees would leave the discharge the scope machine already wrote behind it,
    -- closing the file before the first line is read.
    local text = named(path, "path", 2)
    local output = ffi.new("uint64_t[1]")
    local status = C.nuppNativeV2FileOpen(slice(text), MODES.r, output)
    if status ~= 0 then
        return nil, ffi.string(C.nuppNativeV2LastError())
    end
    local opened = new File(_handle = output[0], _closed = false)
    local into = ffi.new<uint8[?]>(READ_SIZE)
    local held = ""
    local finished = false
    local function trimmed(line: string): string
        if line:sub(-1) == "\r" then
            return line:sub(1, -2)
        end

        return line
    end

    return function(): string?
        if finished then
            return nil
        end
        while true do
            local stop = held:find("\n", 1, true)
            if stop ~= nil then
                local line = held:sub(1, (stop as integer) - 1)
                held = held:sub((stop as integer) + 1)

                return trimmed(line)
            end
            local chunk, why = readBytes(opened._handle, into, READ_SIZE)
            if chunk == nil then
                -- A failed read is not the end of the file, and the iterator has no
                -- error channel to answer through, so finishing cleanly here would
                -- silently truncate the file. The file is closed and the reason
                -- raised instead.
                finished = true
                closeFile(opened)
                error("nupp: io.files lines could not read: " .. (why ?? "unknown reason"), 2)
            elseif chunk == "" then
                finished = true
                closeFile(opened)
                if #held > 0 then
                    local line = held
                    held = ""

                    return trimmed(line)
                end

                return nil
            end
            held = held .. (chunk as string)
        end
    end, nil
end

--- Creates a uniquely named empty file and hands over the obligation to remove or
--- persist it.
---
--- The file is created rather than merely proposed, so no second caller can take the
--- name in between.
---
--- Closing the returned path removes it. `persist` settles it instead, which is
--- what makes the pair a replacement for writing over a destination in place.
---
--- #### Examples
---
--- Write somewhere nobody else can take, then move it into place:
---
--- ```nupp
--- do
---     local scratch = assert(nupp.io.files.createTemporaryFile({directory = "build"}))
---     assert(nupp.io.files.write(scratch:toString(), bytes))
---     assert(scratch:persist("build/output.bin"))
--- end
--- ```
--- @param options where to create it and how to name it
--- @return the created path, or nil on failure
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function createTemporaryFile(options: TemporaryOptions?): (affine(TemporaryPath?, destroyOwner), string?)
    local text, reason = temporary(options, false, 2)
    if text == nil then
        return nil, reason
    end

    return new TemporaryPath(_text = text as string, _directory = false, _closed = false), nil
end

--- Creates a uniquely named empty directory and hands over the obligation to remove or
--- persist it.
--- @param options where to create it and how to name it
--- @return the created path, or nil on failure
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function createTemporaryDirectory(options: TemporaryOptions?): (affine(TemporaryPath?, destroyOwner), string?)
    local text, reason = temporary(options, true, 2)
    if text == nil then
        return nil, reason
    end

    return new TemporaryPath(_text = text as string, _directory = true, _closed = false), nil
end

--- Reads the process's current working directory.
--- @return the current directory, or nil on failure
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @export
export function currentDirectory(): (string?, string?)
    requireFiles()
    local output = ffi.new("uint64_t[1]")

    return answer(C.nuppNativeV2FilesCurrentDirectory(output), output)
end

--- Answers a well-known user folder.
--- @param which the folder to locate
--- @return the folder, or nil when the platform has no such folder
--- @return a failure reason, when unsuccessful
--- @raises when which names no user folder
--- @export
export function userFolder(which: UserFolder): (string?, string?)
    local index = FOLDERS[which]
    if index == nil then
        error("nupp: io.files has no user folder named " .. tostring(which), 2)
    end

    requireFiles()
    local output = ffi.new("uint64_t[1]")

    return answer(C.nuppNativeV2FilesUserFolder(index, output), output)
end
